#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c97e840-f296-503d-88e5-9cf4fc8a4408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362099} {:answer/id #uuid "404a9778-2b59-5121-8fbb-9423bf284569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362100} {:answer/id #uuid "652acd58-20e1-5135-b4af-84b85fa6aabd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362101} {:answer/id #uuid "7791ebe8-5281-5de1-931d-24d8361211ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362102} {:answer/id #uuid "d92be062-f23a-5ae3-9bab-6ce2dec4818c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "652acd58-20e1-5135-b4af-84b85fa6aabd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362101} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8e5837-811c-5c91-a54d-f7aaf6cfb9ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "456bde2b-ef1e-54aa-8da0-4de187218c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-156192" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{8}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{-8}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{3}) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-8}^{8}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-8}^{8}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-8}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{8}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-8}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-8}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cabc1eeb-9608-5e71-8f52-686ab9f527d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328399} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd634da-f8ce-502c-8146-c98df7aedab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328400} {:answer/id #uuid "a91ce62d-50ff-5e6b-8bd4-db84f4c3ee69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328401} {:answer/id #uuid "cef42c49-bc5b-55eb-9d95-099a16363be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328402} {:answer/id #uuid "31bf39b8-f324-513e-86e0-acf8fd3305f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31bf39b8-f324-513e-86e0-acf8fd3305f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328403} :answer-field/id #uuid "38e291dc-eede-5cbe-8cb1-556d4ae6ff7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9337ccd-0531-5f2d-96df-8781c1aaac04" :question/math-academy-id "q-156690" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-x)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be6f67a-d2ec-5bc9-a9cd-458f72abdb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4be6f67a-d2ec-5bc9-a9cd-458f72abdb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362106} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c041021-1d80-557e-893e-8b852062da80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad0b6114-b30d-54df-8d07-1628e7d83ec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-316128" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f05c1cc-9601-58e1-b22b-0c9137e0eba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291871} {:answer/id #uuid "efce043f-c81a-5694-8512-ac98dc0580d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291872} {:answer/id #uuid "71b9adf1-11c1-506e-a9c7-fc16dc13129e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291873} {:answer/id #uuid "3b36ea2c-51c5-55cb-9634-5f1fc7cf3ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291874} {:answer/id #uuid "89c5a5f4-5603-5cf5-ba87-8d510cdf568f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b9adf1-11c1-506e-a9c7-fc16dc13129e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291873} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8acd680-1834-52d3-a1e2-a9009635a9d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5b8f26e-0fd6-5a4b-9564-e6d55b96cd5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88689" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17220ac1-9331-5b65-9cb9-280c772288bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293982} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8f2890-f74d-59ee-94bd-a52d5ca0ebf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293983} {:answer/id #uuid "9f635f75-c4fb-5c76-b9ed-2e6e1c02f6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293984} {:answer/id #uuid "eeab11c1-1365-5363-961e-7eb815d63140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293985} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaf3502-3c8c-5971-84f1-5fb372470c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeab11c1-1365-5363-961e-7eb815d63140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293985} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2328d7f-6c9b-5526-af03-aca8958dcf1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72674b4f-6b99-5086-b3af-6306160b6663" :question/math-academy-id "q-88692" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
